## 3. CHARACTER, TYPOGRAPHY AND TEXT UNIFORMITY AUDIT

This is a critical requirement and must be tested independently across the entire website.

Compare the PC preview against every responsive version, section by section.

### A. Exact character consistency

Verify that all text uses the same intended characters across devices, including:

- Uppercase and lowercase letters.
- Numbers and numerical formatting.
- Punctuation marks.
- Apostrophes and quotation marks.
- Hyphens, en dashes, and em dashes.
- Special characters and symbols.
- Currency symbols and mathematical characters.
- Registered trademarks and other brand-specific symbols where present.
- Accented characters and non-English characters where applicable.
- Unicode characters, decorative symbols, and icon glyphs.

Investigate any character that appears different, missing, substituted, incorrectly encoded, or visually inconsistent on mobile.

Check for encoding issues, unsupported glyphs, missing font weights, fallback fonts, incorrect font declarations, and CSS rules that alter text rendering.

Do not replace a special character with a plain-text approximation unless the original character cannot be supported and an explicitly approved alternative is necessary.

### B. Exact text-content consistency

Verify that all essential text remains identical to the approved PC version.

Check every heading, paragraph, navigation item, label, caption, button, and text-based interface element.

Correct:
- Missing words or letters.
- Incorrect spelling.
- Truncated text.
- Unexpected text substitutions.
- Incorrect capitalization.
- Missing punctuation.
- Inconsistent wording between breakpoints.
- Text hidden by overflow rules.
- Content conditionally removed on mobile.
- Text duplicated or rendered more than once.
- Text that changes unexpectedly when the viewport changes.

Responsive CSS must never silently remove essential information.

Do not shorten headlines, abbreviate words, remove phrases, or replace original copy simply because the mobile screen is smaller.

If text needs more space, improve the layout, adjust appropriate spacing, allow additional lines, or reposition surrounding elements.

### C. Font and typography consistency

Identify the actual approved fonts and weights used in the PC preview.

Ensure that all devices use the same intended:
- Font family.
- Font files and font loading configuration.
- Font weight.
- Font style.
- Letter spacing.
- Text transformation.
- Text colour.
- Intended typographic character and personality.

Inspect whether mobile media queries unintentionally override fonts or font weights.

Verify that the correct font files load successfully on mobile Safari, Android browsers, tablets, and desktop browsers.

Where a font does not contain a required glyph, investigate the font's character coverage and use an appropriate, deliberate fallback only where necessary. Do not allow random system-font substitutions to alter the design.

Typography may scale responsively to maintain readability, but it must retain the same font identity and visual character.

### D. Automated text comparison

Where technically feasible, extract the text from the rendered desktop and mobile pages and compare corresponding content.

Flag differences in:
- Text content.
- Character sequences.
- Heading hierarchy.
- Missing or duplicated text.
- Unexpectedly hidden elements.

Ignore legitimate visual differences such as line wrapping and responsive spacing when comparing content.

Produce a discrepancy list and correct every unintended difference.

